    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d62">

                <pb facs="0072" n="60" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg60"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.248/616: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Afghanistan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Thayer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kabul</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-07-02T12:00:00+04:00">July 2,
                            1942—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received July 3—9:31 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>21. Department’s 6, June 26, 10 p.m. Last night I asked Foreign Minister, whether
                    in view of fact that no reply had been received to Legation’s note of June 25
                    expressing regret over airplane incident, the case might be considered closed.
                    He replied in negative adding that his Government held British Government
                    responsible in such cases and that he would not be able to give me Afghan
                    Government’s answer until he had had explanation from British Government which
                    he hoped to have upon return to Kabul in about 10 days of British Minister who
                    is now in New Delhi. The latter to whom I sent copy of Legation’s note informed
                    me before his departure that Foreign Minister after receiving my note had asked
                        <pb facs="0073" n="61" xml:id="pg61"/> him for British Government’s views on
                    incident. British Minister answered that he would take matter up in New
                    Delhi.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thayer</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
